/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166854 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166854 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166854 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166854 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166854 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166862 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1045 "Velocity and Acceleration as Functions of Displacement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167007 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "775b7c3e-3e96-5ea8-a95f-883523a63b42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167089 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/a8/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167722 #edb/ref 1061 33520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af56a6fb-d731-5179-b8b7-de268aeb13f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167727 #edb/ref 1061 33782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea23ed7c-763e-54fa-af53-bdcd85190355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167728 #edb/ref 1061 33783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87d6c80e-5ac1-5210-accd-0df7f165185b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167746 #edb/ref 1061 35398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a283e70-969e-5e03-8f63-5c695f48086d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168109 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis with acceleration $a(t),$ velocity $v(t)$ and displacement $x(t),$ where $t$ is the time. If we want to calculate the acceleration of $P,$ we use the relationship \n\n$$\na(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we know the acceleration and we want to find the velocity, we use the relationship\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\int a(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nIn both of these relationships, we require $a$ and $v$ to be expressed as functions of $t.$ \n\nNow suppose that we're given the acceleration as a function of the displacement $x.$ How do we calculate the velocity in this case?\n\nFor example, suppose that the acceleration of a particle is given by $a(x) = 4x\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$ A sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ equals $4x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite the left-hand side of this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ by definition, we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nAlso, by the chain rule for differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = 4x.\n$$\n\nBy solving this differential equation, we can find an expression for the velocity $v$ in terms of displacement $x.$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168109 #edb/ref 1065 5369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168109 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "291c6fee-a1ab-554d-b0b8-5508bd23eb2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168109 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253680 #edb/ref 1080 "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253680 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253680 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "016a3368-f9d5-58aa-ab05-aad63edb1b18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253693 #edb/ref 1080 "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253693 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re