 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186255702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256249 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b673d4fe-7d5a-50cb-99f3-481a645a95f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186254246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f893dcb4-30ee-50df-9369-932bd543c3cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186255322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256362 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcd66982-5b26-55ef-acff-14d539285859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2aa7557-417c-5f06-bca6-b63a94c74ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256600 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/47/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "226fe9ff-a963-5e5f-900b-474a261bf3ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256601 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/76/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a56f0b7-8350-56d0-8d3b-b2d932d38f5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256617 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/0d/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d544f0e-e224-5a8f-b2d9-7d23deb9a689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256621 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/3c/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1045 "Velocity and Acceleration as Functions of Displacement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186420431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420428 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "058e9265-ff9b-5baf-8f41-85e7c7868e51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420429 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03c63cb6-dfb8-5730-962f-2e6452481748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420429 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186256625 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
